The resolver uses `PreferGo: true` and a custom Dial. With PreferGo, Go's resolver reads /etc/resolv.conf and queries servers in order with its own timeouts (5s default per query, 2 attempts). The dead IPv6 server adds a delay.

The fix in our control: give the resolver a bounded dial so an unreachable nameserver fails fast, letting the resolver fall back to the next server immediately.

`Dial` returns a net.Conn. We can set a deadline on the returned conn — the standard trick is:

```go
Dial: func(ctx context.Context, network, address string) (net.Conn, error) {
    ...
    conn, err := dial(ctx, network, address)
    if err != nil { return nil, err }
    // Bound the query itself so an unreachable nameserver fails fast and the
    // resolver moves to the next one.
    if deadline := ...; !deadline.IsZero() {
        _ = conn.SetDeadline(deadline)
    }
    return conn, nil
}
```

That's the documented Go idiom (from net.Resolver docs example). We can use `ctx` deadline if set, plus our own DNS query timeout.

